## 📌 Quick Practical Tips for All Routes

| Topic | Advice |
|-------|--------|
| **Transport** | Madrid’s Metro is the fastest way to move between districts. A **Tourist Travel Pass** (10‑trip ticket) costs €12.20 and works on Metro, bus, and Cercanías (zone A). |
| **Ticket Savings** | Most major museums (Prado, Reina Sofía, Thyssen) are **free after 18:00 on weekdays**. Plan your visit accordingly if you’re on a budget. |
| **Peak Hours** | 11:00‑13:00 and 16:00‑18:00 are busiest. Arriving 30 minutes early helps you beat the lines, especially at the Royal Palace and Prado. |
| **Language** | All attractions have English signage and audio guides. Free city Wi‑Fi (Madrid Wi‑Fi) works in most public squares and museums. |
| **Safety** | Madrid is very safe, but keep an eye on belongings in crowded spots (Plaza Mayor, Gran Vía). Pickpocket incidents are most common near metro stations. |
| **Weather** | Late spring (May‑June) and early autumn (Sept‑Oct) give pleasant 15‑25 °C temperatures and fewer tourists. Pack a light jacket for evenings, especially at the Temple of Debod or the cable‑car. |

